course: dictionary
lesson: 4125
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one you need. «Glaze» can mean a shiny coat on a pot, a shiny coat on food, or fitting glass into a window. Ask yourself what the sentence is about, then choose the meaning that fits the topic.
explain: «A shiny glaze, which is a thin coat of glass, makes a mug waterproof.» The mug tells you this glaze is for pottery, not for cake. Test it: put your meaning into the sentence, and if it still makes sense, you have chosen well.
explain: Look at the words around the word as well. «Double-glazed windows have two sheets, or panes, of glass with a layer of trapped air between them.» Double-glazed and windows tell you glaze is a verb here, meaning to fit glass into something. Eyes that «glaze over» are a different meaning again: they look dull and blank.
fact: «Glaze» can be a noun (a glaze) or a verb (to glaze, glazes, glazed, glazing).
fact: A pottery glaze is a thin, shiny glass coating that makes clay smooth, coloured and waterproof.
fact: Pottery is clay baked in a kiln at a very high heat, which makes it hard for good.
fact: «Double glazing» means windows with two layers of glass and trapped air between them.
fact: «Glaze over» describes eyes that go dull and blank because you are bored or tired.
exercise: «A shiny glaze, which is a thin coat of glass, makes a mug waterproof.» What is a glaze in this sentence? | A small handle on a jug | A bowl for mixing dough | A crack in a clay pot | *A shiny coating on pottery
exercise: «Double-glazed windows have two sheets, or panes, of glass.» In this sentence, what does glazed mean? | Gone dull and blank | Covered in sugar and egg | *Fitted with glass | Baked in a kiln
exercise: Ruby says her eyes «glazed over» in the long history lesson. What does this tell you? | She had a shiny coat of sugar on her face | *She was bored or tired | She was delighted and excited | She could see very clearly
test: In cooking, a baker brushes beaten egg onto pastry before baking. Which meaning of glaze is this? | To go dull and blank | To fit glass into a window | A shiny coat brushed onto food | *A shiny coat brushed onto food, which makes the pastry golden and shiny
test: Oscar says glaze means glass in every sentence. Which sentence shows he is wrong? | A thin coat of glass makes a mug waterproof. | *Oscar brushed a sugar glaze onto the doughnuts. | Pottery is baked in a kiln. | Double-glazed windows keep the heat in.
exam: When a word has more than one meaning, which clues help you choose the right one? | The number of letters in the word | *The words around it and what the sentence is about | The colour of the page it is printed on | Whether the word is written in capital letters
